ADR 0011 decides that template dict literals and engine-built objects are byte-identical / 1:1 with JS. JS orders own keys array-index keys first (ascending numeric), then string keys by insertion (OrdinaryOwnPropertyKeys). The port iterates Hash::Ordered in pure insertion order (Lib::keys, lib/Text/Nunjucks/Lib.pm ~360), so integer-like keys diverge without an ADR.

Oracle vs port (k34 fidelity audit, 2026-11-13):
- {% for k, v in {"b":1, "2":2, "1":3} %}{{k}};{% endfor %} -> oracle 1;2;b; port b;2;1;
- keys a, 10, 9, 01, -1, 4294967295, 4294967294, 1.5 -> oracle 9;10;4294967294;a;01;-1;4294967295;1.5; port a;10;9;01;-1;4294967295;4294967294;1.5;
- dump of the first literal -> {"1":3,"2":2,"b":1}
- groupby over years: 9|10|2018|2019|b|a (k34: 2 assertions in t/upstream/filters.t TODO on this card)

Fix in the one ordering seam (covers for, dump, list, dictsort, groupby result), with oracle corpus cases for the probes above. Open part for the maintainer (k17 note A): should plain caller hashes, sorted per ADR 0011, also put array-index keys first numerically (today 10 sorts before 9)?

